// swag global type overrides (swag --overridesFile .swaggo).
//
// ⛔ Only types swag cannot resolve on its own belong here. Every line is a place
// where the generated schema is *less* true than the Go type, so each one needs a
// reason — an override nobody can argue with is how a spec starts lying.
//
// json.RawMessage: swag v2.0.0-rc6 cannot resolve it at all ("cannot find type
// definition: json.RawMessage") and refuses to generate, so this is not a choice
// between a good schema and a better one — it is the difference between a spec and
// no spec. ⚠️ The rendered schema is `{"type":"object"}`, which is **wrong for the
// fields that hold a scalar**: handler.TemplateVarResponse.Default is a
// RawMessage precisely to preserve "string / number / bool / array" (see its
// comment). A generated SDK will therefore type those as objects. ⛔ Do not fix
// that by changing the Go type — the wire shape is the contract; fix it, if it is
// worth fixing, by giving those fields a named type with a truthful schema.
//
// ⚠️ The short spelling is the one that works. `replace encoding/json.RawMessage
// interface{}` (the fully-qualified form the swag docs show) is accepted and then
// does nothing — measured on rc6: generation still fails on the same type.
replace json.RawMessage interface{}

// time.Duration: same failure mode as above — swag v2.0.0-rc6 answers "cannot
// find type definition: time.Duration" and **refuses to generate at all**, so
// without this line the settings group (nine snapshots, eighteen routes) takes
// the whole document down with it.
//
// ⭐ Unlike the line above, this override is not a loss. time.Duration *is* an
// int64, none of the snapshot types define MarshalJSON, and encoding/json
// therefore puts a plain JSON number on the wire and reads one back. `int64` is
// what the wire actually carries, so the generated schema is exactly true.
//
// ⚠️ What it cannot carry is the unit: the number is **nanoseconds**, so 30s is
// 30000000000 and a client that sends 30 has asked for 30ns. OpenAPI has no
// vocabulary for a unit on an integer, so that fact lives in the @Description of
// each settings endpoint instead of being silently lost here. ⛔ Do not "fix"
// this by replacing with `string` — that would describe a wire format the
// daemon does not accept.
replace time.Duration int64

// pq.StringArray: third type in the same family — swag v2.0.0-rc6 cannot resolve
// it and refuses to generate, which takes the whole document down as soon as any
// endpoint returns a types.Rule (AppliedTo is a pq.StringArray).
//
// ⭐ Also not a loss. `type StringArray []string`, and lib/pq gives it Scan and
// Value for database/sql but NO MarshalJSON/UnmarshalJSON (checked in the module
// cache), so encoding/json treats it as a plain []string in both directions.
// `[]string` is exactly what the wire carries.
//
// ⚠️ The Postgres array literal syntax it exists for ({a,b}) never appears in
// JSON — that encoding lives on the database side of the struct, behind the
// gorm tag, and is not part of the API contract.
replace pq.StringArray []string
